-- title:
--   Counting homomorphisms into a cyclic group killing X
-- statement:
--   Let $X$ and $Y$ be types in the lowest universe, each carrying the structure of an additive commutative group, each finite, with $Y$ additively cyclic (generated by a single element). Assume that $X$ is annihilated by the order of $Y$: for every $x \in X$ one has $\operatorname{Nat.card}(Y) \cdot x = 0$. Then the number of additive group homomorphisms $X \to Y$ equals the cardinality of $X$, i.e. $\operatorname{Nat.card}(X \to_{+} Y) = \operatorname{Nat.card}(X)$. Here $\operatorname{Nat.card}$ is the natural-number cardinality, and no pairing or duality map is asserted — only the equality of the two finite cardinalities.
--
--   This is the elementary count $|\operatorname{Hom}(X,Y)| = |X|$ for a finite abelian group $X$ whose exponent divides the order of a finite cyclic group $Y$, the case of Pontryagin-style duality for finite abelian groups with cyclic target. It is used in the Tate-cohomology computations of the project, where it supplies the cardinality of a group of homomorphisms from a Tate cohomology group into a cyclic cohomology group; it is cited by [`ZMod.card_addMonoidHom_pi_eq_and_bichar_pow_val_of_isUnit_one_sub_pow`](thm.html#ZMod.card_addMonoidHom_pi_eq_and_bichar_pow_val_of_isUnit_one_sub_pow).
